/**
\page MxNConceptPage MxN Multi-Widget Concept

\tableofcontents


\section MxNConceptPage_Introduction Introduction to the MxN View

The MxN MultiWidget View allows users to freely arrange as many render windows as they
like in arbitrary arrangements. These windows are linked with each other in so-called
"Synchronization Groups" (short: "Sync Groups") that share node-selections and -properties
such as visibility and layer (i.e. the ordering which node is in front of which).
For more detailed information on how to use it, take a look at \ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget help page.
Here, we will give an overview of the most relevant classes for the MxN View.


\image html MxNClassesOverview.png "Overview of MxN related classes" width=60%
\image latex MxNClassesOverview.png "Overview of MxN related classes" width=60%


\section MxNConceptPage_MxNWidget MxN Multi-Widget

The \ref QmitkMxNMultiWidget is the main class for the MxN View, taking care of organizing
the render windows and sync groups. It creates new render windows when necessary and extends
them with a \ref QmitkRenderWindowUtilityWidget, which it connects to for signals if the
window's sync group changes.
To offload the synchronization of different windows, it uses \ref QmitkSynchronizedWidgetConnector,
one for each sync group.


\section MxNConceptPage_UtilityWidget Utility Widget

Unlike in the standard 4-window-view, in the MxN View each \ref QmitkRenderWindowWidget is
extended with a \ref QmitkRenderWindowUtilityWidget. This widget takes the form of a bar
at the top of the render window, with features including:
 - Selecting a view direction (i.e. "sagittal", "axial", "coronal")
 - Navigating along the current view axis via a \ref QmitkSliceNavigationWidget
 - Selecting a synchronization group
 - A \ref QmitkSynchronizedNodeSelectionWidget to change the shown selection of nodes, as well as their properties

When the sync group of a render window is changed, this signal will inform the \ref MxNConceptPage_MxNWidget
of the change to handle the synchronization.


\section MxNConceptPage_SynchronizedNodeSelectionWidget Synchronized Node Selection Widget

To select and modify the data shown in each render window, their \ref MxNConceptPage_UtilityWidget contains
a \ref QmitkSynchronizedNodeSelectionWidget. In it, one can change the current selection of nodes,
as well as their visibility and ordering. This state is synchronized with every other render window
that is part of the same sync group, as specified in their utility widgets.
Changes in the selection of nodes or their visibility are directly signalled from the \ref QmitkSynchronizedNodeSelectionWidget,
while changes in the node order (i.e. their layers) are emitted by the contained \ref QmitkRenderWindowDataNodeTableModel.
Each Synchronized Node Selection widget has exactly <i>one</i> data model that takes care of its
selection and node properties.


\section MxNConceptPage_SynchronizedWidgetConnector Synchronized Widget Connector

A \ref QmitkSynchronizedWidgetConnector takes care of synchronizing the widgets belonging to one
sync group. New widgets can be added via
`QmitkSynchronizedWidgetConnector::ConnectWidget(const QmitkSynchronizedNodeSelectionWidget* nodeSelectionWidget)`
and removed via
` QmitkSynchronizedWidgetConnector::DisconnectWidget(const QmitkSynchronizedNodeSelectionWidget* nodeSelectionWidget)`.
Connected \ref QmitkSynchronizedNodeSelectionWidget will inform each other of changes to their
node selections and their visibility / ordering. To get newly connected widgets up to date, the
\ref QmitkSynchronizedWidgetConnector internally saves the current selection and selection mode.


\section MxNConceptPage_LayoutFormat Layout file format

The on-disk layout files (`mxnLayout_*.json`), the documents accepted by
`QmitkMxNMultiWidget::ApplyLayout`, and the documents emitted by
`QmitkMxNMultiWidget::SerializeLayout` all share a single JSON format. The
human-friendly walkthrough lives on a dedicated page:
\ref MxNLayoutFormatPage. The closed, normative reference is the JSON Schema
`Modules/QtWidgets/resource/mxn-layout-v2.schema.json`.


\section MxNConceptPage_PluginEditors Plugin-owned MxN editors

A plugin can ship its own MxN editor, running next to the stock MxN display without
sharing its state and without the generic MxN tooling taking it over. It does not
subclass anything: it declares a new editor ID for the stock editor class
`QmitkMxNMultiWidgetEditor` and drives the editor's \ref QmitkMxNMultiWidget from its
own view. The example plugin `org.mitk.example.gui.mxneditor`
(\ref BlueBerryExampleMxNEditor) is the complete, compiled reference. Start it from the
`BlueBerryExampleLauncher` with the `MxNEditor` configuration.

\subsection MxNConceptPage_PluginEditors_Declaration Declaration

\code{.xml}
<extension point="org.blueberry.ui.editors">
  <editor id="org.mitk.editors.longitrack" name="LongiTrack Display">
    <class class="QmitkMxNMultiWidgetEditor">
      <parameter name="layoutLock" value="structure,direction,proportions,content"/>
      <parameter name="initialLayout" value="resources/LongiTrackLayout.json"/>
    </class>
  </editor>
</extension>
\endcode

The declaring plugin lists `org.mitk.gui.qt.mxnmultiwidgeteditor` in its `Require-Plugin`.
All parameters are optional:
 - `widgetName`: the prefix of the editor's render-window IDs, `<widgetName>__<cellId>`.
   It defaults to the editor ID, which keeps the names unique without a naming rule. It
   must match `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9.-]*$`. An override must be unique among the MxN
   editors open at the same time; this is not enforced. The stock editor sets `mxn`.
 - `layoutLock`: a comma-separated list of the aspects users cannot change
   (see \ref MxNConceptPage_PluginEditors_Locks).
 - `initialLayout`: a layout file (\ref MxNLayoutFormatPage) embedded in the declaring
   plugin through `CACHED_RESOURCE_FILES`, with a path relative to that plugin, like the
   `icon` attribute. Its window IDs must carry the editor's widget name. It is applied
   before the editor is first shown, so the first frame already shows it.

Do not declare `extensions`: an editor declaring them can become the default editor for
those files.

A broken declaration does not fail silently. This covers an unknown parameter, an
invalid name or lock token, and an initial layout that is missing, not valid JSON, or
rejected by `ApplyLayout`. The editor opens with an error panel that names the plugin,
the parameter and the problem, and logs the same message. No MxN widget of that editor
stays alive.

\subsection MxNConceptPage_PluginEditors_Code Declaration and code

The declared locks and layout are starting values. `QmitkMxNMultiWidget::SetLayoutLocks`
and `QmitkMxNMultiWidget::ApplyLayout` change them at any time, and `LayoutLocksChanged`
reports lock changes. Declare what is static, so the first frame is right, and use code
for what depends on the data or on the workflow, for example a layout that depends on the
number of time points.

The view finds the editor's widget by editor ID (`FindEditors` with `MATCH_ID`). It holds
the widget only as a guarded pointer and looks it up again on part events, because every
reopened editor builds a new widget. Open the editor with `MATCH_ID` too: an editor input
of the data storage alone would also match an open stock MxN editor. For the same reason,
opening data while the plugin's editor is the active one shows the data there instead of
raising the stock MxN editor.

\subsection MxNConceptPage_PluginEditors_Locks Layout locks

A lock withdraws one aspect from users:
 - `structure`: the grid, the synchronization and selection groups, the sync links and
   layout loading. The cells' sync barcodes do not open the MxN Layout Editor, and the view
   does not bind to the editor; it says that the plugin manages the layout instead.
 - `direction`: a cell's view direction, from the cell's own context menu and plane label.
 - `proportions`: the splitter handles between cells.
 - `content`: the data a cell shows through its own Data button and "Data..." entry, which
   would also change the cell's selection group.

Only `structure` keeps the MxN Layout Editor view away. The other three locks cover the
cell's own controls; while `structure` is unlocked, the view's grid changes, data-based
layouts and layout loads still replace directions, proportions and content. Lock
`structure` as well when these must stay fixed.

Locks never block programmatic calls: `ApplyLayout`, `SetSyncLink`, `SetCellSelectionGroup`,
`SetViewDirection`, `SetMaximizedCell` and a cell's node selection all keep working, so the
owning plugin keeps full control. Maximizing a cell stays available to users.
Views that act on whichever editor is active are not covered: the Render Window Manager
can still change per-window visibility and layers, and the Data Manager changes node
properties globally.

\subsection MxNConceptPage_PluginEditors_Preferences Preferences

Each MxN editor reads the preference node named after its own editor ID, so a plugin
editor starts from defaults and does not follow the user's stock MxN settings. To expose
its display preferences, a plugin declares a page with the stock page class and an
`editorId`:

\code{.xml}
<extension point="org.blueberry.ui.preferencePages">
  <page id="org.mitk.LongiTrackDisplayPreferencePage" name="Display"
        category="org.mitk.LongiTrackPreferencePage">
    <class class="QmitkMxNMultiWidgetEditorPreferencePage">
      <parameter name="editorId" value="org.mitk.editors.longitrack"/>
    </class>
    <keywordreference id="org.mitk.MxNMultiWidgetEditorPreferencePageKeywords"/>
  </page>
</extension>
\endcode

The page's Reset clears every key at the root of the editor's node. Keep a plugin's own
settings in a child node of the editor node (e.g. `org.mitk.editors.longitrack/backend`)
or in the plugin's own node, never as root keys of the editor node.

\subsection MxNConceptPage_PluginEditors_Tooling Generic tooling

 - The MxN Layout Editor view binds to an MxN editor when it becomes visible, not when it
   is activated. With two editors side by side, clicking from one into the other does not
   rebind the view.
 - MovieMaker disables itself for every MxN editor, as for the stock one.
 - Views that follow the active editor, such as Segmentation and the Render Window Manager,
   work on the plugin's editor like on any other.
 - The REST API does not see a second MxN editor: its editor list and the
   `/rendering/editors/mxn/...` endpoints address the stock editor only.
 - A slice scroll in one editor re-renders the visible windows of the other one as well.
   The state stays separate.

*/